From f8efad488a98f2d2ef54b535a62008bf0d5354b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vasilii Zolotukhin <vasilii.zolotukhin@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 11:23:50 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] Mac: one tab strip with the tabs of all profiles

In every profile window (also one that is alone, so tabs always look
the same) Chromium's own tab strip is made invisible and inert (it keeps its row
and the window's minimum size, and leaves the accessibility tree) and a
unified tab strip covers it. The unified strip shows the tabs of all
profile windows in one global order, all drawn the same way, with a small
triangle in their profile's theme color in the top-left corner: click activates (switching
profile windows as needed), the close button or a middle click closes,
dragging reorders anywhere, the new tab button opens a tab in the
current profile (right click: in any profile), and the tab menu has
reload, duplicate, mute, close, close others and "Move to profile"
(reopens the URL in another profile in the same place).

Within a window the global order always follows Chromium's tab order,
so session restore and extensions keep working; the global order only
decides how the windows' tabs interleave. It is saved in the
org.chromium.Chromium.profile-tabs defaults domain (per user data dir)
and applied to the windows restored at startup. Chrome's tab selection
and move shortcuts (Cmd+1...9, Ctrl+Tab, Cmd+Opt+arrows, Cmd+Shift+[/],
Ctrl+PageUp/PageDown, Ctrl+Shift+PageUp/PageDown) follow the global
order.

In immersive fullscreen the unified strip moves along with Chromium's
tab strip into the tab overlay widget and back.

The strip lives in the new unified_tab_strip_mac.{h,mm}; the frame view
hit test hook now returns HTCAPTION for empty strip space.
